WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:01.089
Iraq.

00:00:01.089 --> 00:00:05.530
I’m sure that many of you automatically
associate this word, this name, with a destroyed

00:00:05.530 --> 00:00:09.660
country, ravaged by war.

00:00:09.660 --> 00:00:14.639
A place with no hope or future, a place where
religious extremism runs wild and there are

00:00:14.639 --> 00:00:19.630
endless conflicts between Sunnis and Shiites.

00:00:19.630 --> 00:00:20.770
And you know what?

00:00:20.770 --> 00:00:21.870
You’re absolutely right.

00:00:21.870 --> 00:00:25.930
This has been Iraq’s reality for the last
few decades, especially following the First

00:00:25.930 --> 00:00:31.230
Gulf War, the US invasion and the emergence
of the Islamic State – the terrorist group

00:00:31.230 --> 00:00:35.420
which ended up occupying a large part of the
country.

00:00:35.420 --> 00:00:40.649
We’re talking about a place that is a factory
for bad news.

00:00:40.649 --> 00:00:44.719
Without delving too deep, in the last few
months, strong social revolts have caused

00:00:44.719 --> 00:00:45.790
a lot of tension.

00:00:45.790 --> 00:00:48.590
The Iraqis are unhappy and... with good reason.

00:00:48.590 --> 00:00:54.480
(AUDIO: 10/27/2019 Iraq: At least 42 dead
and 2,000 injured in anti-government protests.

00:00:54.480 --> 00:00:55.680
SKY NEWS)
https://news.sky.com/story/iraq-at-least-42-dead-and-2-000-injured-in-anti-government-protests-11845519

00:00:55.680 --> 00:01:00.020
(AUDIO: Iraq protests: Capital Baghdad blocked
as unrest escalates.

00:01:00.020 --> 00:01:03.368
Demonstrators have blocked the main streets
of Iraq’s, Baghdad, while massive protests

00:01:03.368 --> 00:01:04.368
continue against the government.

00:01:04.368 --> 00:01:05.368
BBC)
world-middle-east-50280498

00:01:05.368 --> 00:01:07.389
However, this time we’re not going to talk
about a destroyed Iraq, or about disasters

00:01:07.389 --> 00:01:11.159
or victims, or misfortune.

00:01:11.159 --> 00:01:15.479
You must have realised by now that we like
looking at the bright side of things.

00:01:15.479 --> 00:01:21.320
And it’s precisely in this video, here on
VISUALPOLITIK, that we’ll try to show the

00:01:21.320 --> 00:01:24.069
side no one ever talks about regarding Iraq.

00:01:24.069 --> 00:01:26.009
That is, its good side.

00:01:26.009 --> 00:01:27.929
But does this country really have an upside?

00:01:27.929 --> 00:01:31.880
What would you think if I told you that even
though this country still has loads of problems

00:01:31.880 --> 00:01:35.950
and is light years away from being a good
place to live, there are still plenty of reasons

00:01:35.950 --> 00:01:37.709
to be optimistic?

00:01:37.709 --> 00:01:39.139
Well...

00:01:39.139 --> 00:01:41.999
Listen up.

00:01:41.999 --> 00:01:43.429
(INTRO)

00:01:43.429 --> 00:01:48.740
It’s time to… look at some history.

00:01:48.740 --> 00:01:52.270
After all, we cannot understand today's Iraq
without taking a look at their past.

00:01:52.270 --> 00:01:54.680
At least the country’s most recent past.

00:01:54.680 --> 00:01:59.229
In 2003, after Saddam Hussein and his regime
fell following the US invasion, a state of

00:01:59.229 --> 00:02:05.439
chaos and a lack of institutionality emerged
that continues to this day.

00:02:05.439 --> 00:02:10.030
After the US occupation, an interim government
was established for a year and then a transitional

00:02:10.030 --> 00:02:13.560
government was in charge between May 2005
and May 2006.

00:02:13.560 --> 00:02:18.200
It was this transitional government that drafted
the current Constitution.

00:02:18.200 --> 00:02:23.650
However, despite all these attempts for political
normalization, the country's governance was

00:02:23.650 --> 00:02:24.909
virtually impossible.

00:02:24.909 --> 00:02:29.909
And it was precisely in this highly conflictive
environment, with institutions that were unable

00:02:29.909 --> 00:02:35.780
to function and a complete lack of safety,
that jihadist militias increased their power

00:02:35.780 --> 00:02:36.780
and influence.

00:02:36.780 --> 00:02:40.409
That is how extremism boomed, which resulted
in numerous attacks that, of course, caused

00:02:40.409 --> 00:02:44.900
huge amounts of damage and greatly fueled
the anti-Western sentiment amongst a good

00:02:44.900 --> 00:02:46.870
part of the population.

00:02:46.870 --> 00:02:52.599
Of course, the United States and its allies
hadn’t only occupied the country, they were

00:02:52.599 --> 00:02:57.150
unable to bring order to it and had turned
the Iraqi city streets into true hornets’

00:02:57.150 --> 00:02:58.150
nests.

00:02:58.150 --> 00:03:01.269
That’s why the people who perceived international
troops negatively were no longer just the

00:03:01.269 --> 00:03:05.830
supporters of Saddam Hussein’s regime, but
a much wider range of people.

00:03:05.830 --> 00:03:10.930
And it was precisely this rejection of the
west, which grew slowly, that explains news

00:03:10.930 --> 00:03:12.689
stories like this:

00:03:12.689 --> 00:03:18.930
(AUDIO: Iraq: anti-US cleric Moqtada al-Sadr’s
bloc confirmed as election winners.

00:03:18.930 --> 00:03:19.930
- The Guardian)

00:03:19.930 --> 00:03:22.390
Is Iraq at risk of becoming a new Iran?

00:03:22.390 --> 00:03:25.629
Well, let’s not get ahead of ourselves.

00:03:25.629 --> 00:03:31.000
Let’s go back to 2014.

00:03:31.000 --> 00:03:38.510
That year an Iraqi man, Abu Bakr al-Baghdadi,
leader of the Islamic State jihadist group,

00:03:38.510 --> 00:03:42.359
declared his own caliphate, a world caliphate,
on Iraqi lands.

00:03:42.359 --> 00:03:51.439
He came to be considered a successor of Muhammad
and his call mobilized vast numbers of militants.

00:03:51.439 --> 00:03:55.620
The restoration of the caliphate led to the
beginning of a huge military offensive by

00:03:55.620 --> 00:03:59.599
ISIS where heinous crimes were committed while
capturing huge lands.

00:03:59.599 --> 00:04:04.420
Even important Iraqi cities like Mosul or
Fallujah fell under their power.

00:04:04.420 --> 00:04:10.359
The most recent “reign of terror” had
been created.

00:04:10.359 --> 00:04:15.029
Yes, we all know this story.

00:04:15.029 --> 00:04:20.630
Especially now that the United States has
confirmed that it has eliminated Abu Bakr

00:04:20.630 --> 00:04:22.450
al-Baghdadi,

00:04:22.450 --> 00:04:28.060
The fact is that since 2014, the defeat of
ISIS became the number one priority of both

00:04:28.060 --> 00:04:32.360
the Iraqi government – as Iraq was held
against the ropes due to ISIS’s advance

00:04:32.360 --> 00:04:37.319
– and the United States government, as well
as the Kurds, who we discussed in depth in

00:04:37.319 --> 00:04:38.319
this recent video

00:04:38.319 --> 00:04:46.061
And, of course, faced with this challenge,
all of the Iraqi government’s efforts were

00:04:46.061 --> 00:04:48.270
focused on defeating the Islamic State.

00:04:48.270 --> 00:04:50.699
Everything else slipped into the background.

00:04:50.699 --> 00:04:54.870
As you can imagine, this meant Iraq’s social
and economic advances were paralyzed for a

00:04:54.870 --> 00:04:55.870
long time.

00:04:55.870 --> 00:05:00.450
Who would dare to invest in a shattered country
that was also being invaded by ISIS barbarians?

00:05:00.450 --> 00:05:05.060
The worst part is that despite all their efforts
and help from the west, the threat of ISIS

00:05:05.060 --> 00:05:08.550
still hasn’t disappeared even though the
caliphate has been dismantled.

00:05:08.550 --> 00:05:17.979
(AUDIO: ISIS Is Regaining Strength in Iraq
and Syria.

00:05:17.979 --> 00:05:18.979
- The New York Times )

00:05:18.979 --> 00:05:19.979
But just a second.

00:05:19.979 --> 00:05:23.610
At the beginning of this video, we promised
that we wouldn’t talk about Iraq’s worst

00:05:23.610 --> 00:05:25.630
side, but the opposite, right?

00:05:25.630 --> 00:05:30.270
Okay, it's hard to find positive things in
a place like Iraq, but believe me there are

00:05:30.270 --> 00:05:31.270
some.

00:05:31.270 --> 00:05:34.240
The fact is that after the Caliphate’s fall,
Iraqi institutions have been strengthened

00:05:34.240 --> 00:05:36.270
and security has greatly improved.

00:05:36.270 --> 00:05:40.139
This has made Iraq begin to take a few steps
forward.

00:05:40.139 --> 00:05:43.270
Listen up.

00:05:43.270 --> 00:05:46.410
(CHANGES ARRIVE.

00:05:46.410 --> 00:05:48.930
BETTER LATE THAN NEVER)

00:05:48.930 --> 00:05:52.980
I don't think anyone would doubt the difficulty
of rebuilding a country that has practically

00:05:52.980 --> 00:05:54.680
been at war since 1980.

00:05:54.680 --> 00:05:57.780
That’s almost 40 years.

00:05:57.780 --> 00:06:03.150
It’s a situation that, not only didn’t
improve with the US invasion, but actually

00:06:03.150 --> 00:06:04.150
got worse.

00:06:04.150 --> 00:06:08.130
After the occupation, and the violence that
broke out, Baghdad became a city divided by

00:06:08.130 --> 00:06:14.600
large walls, military controls and road-blocks
everywhere.

00:06:14.600 --> 00:06:15.930
Mobility in the city was very, very difficult.

00:06:15.930 --> 00:06:20.599
To give you an idea, it took hours to cross
the city by car because of the huge number

00:06:20.599 --> 00:06:23.100
of detours and controls you had to cross.

00:06:23.100 --> 00:06:27.250
The city looked more like a gigantic military
base than a country’s capital.

00:06:27.250 --> 00:06:29.660
Citizens lived a virtually kidnapped existence.

00:06:29.660 --> 00:06:33.630
Curfews were common and it wasn't even safe
to go out for tea or coffee.

00:06:33.630 --> 00:06:37.669
Baghdad condemned you to a terrible life and
in many cases... a rather short one.

00:06:37.669 --> 00:06:41.610
However, after the defeat of the Islamic State
Caliphate in Iraq, things have begun to improve

00:06:41.610 --> 00:06:43.150
by leaps and bounds.

00:06:43.150 --> 00:06:44.400
Check this out.

00:06:44.400 --> 00:06:50.449
To turn the situation around, one of the key
measures that the Iraqi government has implemented

00:06:50.449 --> 00:06:55.169
has been the plan to eliminate all military
checkpoints, obstacles and interior walls

00:06:55.169 --> 00:06:57.060
that separated neighborhoods from each other.

00:06:57.060 --> 00:07:01.409
After 15 years of living with these elements,
Baghdad’s population has experienced worrying

00:07:01.409 --> 00:07:04.479
social changes, mostly a result of isolation.

00:07:04.479 --> 00:07:08.370
The isolation of the neighborhoods created
closed communities, which barely had contact

00:07:08.370 --> 00:07:10.250
with other communities within the city.

00:07:10.250 --> 00:07:14.979
This led to an increase in violence and rejection
among the different groups once access was

00:07:14.979 --> 00:07:15.979
restored.

00:07:15.979 --> 00:07:19.660
And to combat this, the first step to take
is evident.

00:07:19.660 --> 00:07:20.660
Take a look.

00:07:20.660 --> 00:07:25.150
(AUDIO: Iraq lifts nearly 30 km of blast walls
from Baghdad: official.

00:07:25.150 --> 00:07:26.150
- France 24)

00:07:26.150 --> 00:07:31.759
(AUDIO: Iraq plans to remove 300 checkpoints,
erect security fence in Baghdad.“More than

00:07:31.759 --> 00:07:36.979
600 streets will be opened up in Baghdad and
all concrete blocks will be removed from streets

00:07:36.979 --> 00:07:39.590
in order to ease traffic jam.”

00:07:39.590 --> 00:07:42.319
Iraqi News)

00:07:42.319 --> 00:07:45.610
Imagine how it would’ve been to live in
Baghdad in the last decade.

00:07:45.610 --> 00:07:46.690
A true nightmare…

00:07:46.690 --> 00:07:49.460
But , the changes don't end with the look
of capital.

00:07:49.460 --> 00:07:50.509
What’s happening in other arenas?

00:07:50.509 --> 00:07:51.509
What about Iran’s influence over Iraq?

00:07:51.509 --> 00:07:54.439
Is it as significant as they say?

00:07:54.439 --> 00:08:00.470
Let’s take a look.

00:08:00.470 --> 00:08:05.590
(EMERGING FROM BETWEEN TWO OPPOSITE POLES?)

00:08:05.590 --> 00:08:09.310
Even though it may be hard to believe, there
is more good news on the horizon for Iraq.

00:08:09.310 --> 00:08:11.650
It looks like things are starting to function
again.

00:08:11.650 --> 00:08:15.980
Public works have increased, housing construction
is at its highest level in recent decades

00:08:15.980 --> 00:08:17.930
and crude oil production is sky-rocketing.

00:08:17.930 --> 00:08:21.819
(AUDIO: Iraq’s oil production has nearly
doubled over the past decade.

00:08:21.819 --> 00:08:22.819
- U.S. Energy Information Administration)

00:08:22.819 --> 00:08:26.930
According to OPEC, crude oil exports have
grown to around 3.8 million barrels per day,

00:08:26.930 --> 00:08:30.270
allowing Iraq to make almost 70 billion dollars
in 2018.

00:08:30.270 --> 00:08:35.670
(AUDIO: Iraq is pumping record oil, creating
a ‘fully-blown migraine’ for OPEC’s

00:08:35.670 --> 00:08:36.820
cutting plans)

00:08:36.820 --> 00:08:41.130
The problem stems from the fact that the relationship
between the central government and the semi-autonomous

00:08:41.130 --> 00:08:45.791
region of Iraqi Kurdistan – which also happens
to be where a large part of the deposits can

00:08:45.791 --> 00:08:48.950
be found – is frankly quite bad.

00:08:48.950 --> 00:08:54.070
And in fact, one of the reasons for the tension
is oil.

00:08:54.070 --> 00:08:56.950
In Iraq, oil exports depend heavily on the
Kurdish region.

00:08:56.950 --> 00:09:01.160
The central government and the Kurds share
oil revenues and sometimes this ends up serving

00:09:01.160 --> 00:09:04.950
as a political weapon for each side to put
pressure on the other.

00:09:04.950 --> 00:09:07.580
But this isn’t the main reason for their
bad relationship.

00:09:07.580 --> 00:09:13.010
The real trigger was the Iraqi Kurdistan independence
referendum, which we discussed previously

00:09:13.010 --> 00:09:17.070
here on VISUALPOLITIK.

00:09:17.070 --> 00:09:21.230
Without a doubt, one of the things that has
aroused the most fears and misgivings in investors

00:09:21.230 --> 00:09:25.570
and the international community has been the
relationship between Baghdad and Tehran.

00:09:25.570 --> 00:09:26.570
Yes.

00:09:26.570 --> 00:09:28.390
In Washington they must be pulling their hair
out.

00:09:28.390 --> 00:09:32.520
You invade a country, you spend a fortune,
you have a lot of casualties and in the end

00:09:32.520 --> 00:09:37.480
that country ends up in the hands of one of
your greatest enemies.

00:09:37.480 --> 00:09:39.780
Crazy.

00:09:39.780 --> 00:09:44.630
(AUDIO: Iran-Backed Militias Are In Iraq to
Stay.

00:09:44.630 --> 00:09:45.630
- Foreign Policy)

00:09:45.630 --> 00:09:48.152
(AUDIO: Iran Dominates in Iraq After U.S.
‘Handed the Country Over’.

00:09:48.152 --> 00:09:49.790
- The New York Times)

00:09:49.790 --> 00:09:51.750
So that’s how it is.

00:09:51.750 --> 00:09:56.000
Although to be fair, we should state that
Iran’s influence seems to have lost a bit

00:09:56.000 --> 00:09:57.540
of steam in recent years.

00:09:57.540 --> 00:10:01.330
This may be due to, among other things, enormous
pressure from Saudi Arabia.

00:10:01.330 --> 00:10:05.370
But even so, we’re talking about a relationship
that puts many political leaders around the

00:10:05.370 --> 00:10:07.521
world, especially in Washington, on edge.

00:10:07.521 --> 00:10:14.030
You see, a union comprised of these two countries,
Iran and Iraq joined together, would lead

00:10:14.030 --> 00:10:19.900
to an alliance that would have combined reserves
of more than 300 billion easy-to-exploit oil

00:10:19.900 --> 00:10:22.090
barrels.

00:10:22.090 --> 00:10:25.270
This would be the largest conventional oil
reserve in the world.

00:10:25.270 --> 00:10:27.780
More than the reserves of Saudi Arabia or
Venezuela.

00:10:27.780 --> 00:10:33.100
We’d be talking about an alliance that,
with 120 million inhabitants and such natural

00:10:33.100 --> 00:10:35.990
resources, could easily become the greatest
power of the Middle East.

00:10:35.990 --> 00:10:41.340
What’s more, we’re talking about two Shiite-majority
countries, 2 of the 3 Shiite-majority countries

00:10:41.340 --> 00:10:49.450
in the world – the other one is Azerbaijan.

00:10:49.450 --> 00:10:52.900
And I don't think I need to remind you that,
in this area of ​​the world, religious

00:10:52.900 --> 00:10:54.610
ties are extremely important.

00:10:54.610 --> 00:10:58.820
Well, the fact is that in modern-day Iraq
there are at least 30 armed groups, most of

00:10:58.820 --> 00:11:03.460
them Iran supporters, who make up the so-called
Popular Mobilization Forces.

00:11:03.460 --> 00:11:07.750
These paramilitary forces have no less than
125,000 troops.

00:11:07.750 --> 00:11:12.200
125,000 troops over which Tehran holds a very
important influence.

00:11:12.200 --> 00:11:16.450
And this is something that the Iraqi government
itself has tried to curb...

00:11:16.450 --> 00:11:18.930
But, in all honesty, they haven’t been successful.

00:11:18.930 --> 00:11:23.120
First, the armed groups were invited to leave
their loyalty to Iran in order to join the

00:11:23.120 --> 00:11:27.001
Iraqi Armed Forces and now they’re trying
to do it by force, which is putting the country

00:11:27.001 --> 00:11:28.030
on alert again.

00:11:28.030 --> 00:11:30.320
Getting rid of one only to get saddled with
another.

00:11:30.320 --> 00:11:32.170
Some countries seem doomed.

00:11:32.170 --> 00:11:37.190
(AUDIO: Death tolls soars as pro-Iran militias
accused of shooting protesters.

00:11:37.190 --> 00:11:38.190
- The Independent)

00:11:38.190 --> 00:11:41.390
Now, it was precisely due to this wave of
violence that the Iraqi government is now

00:11:41.390 --> 00:11:45.980
trying to put more distance between themselves
and the Iranian regime and to look more towards

00:11:45.980 --> 00:11:46.980
Riyadh instead.

00:11:46.980 --> 00:11:49.400
That is the Iraqi government’s new strategy.

00:11:49.400 --> 00:11:53.450
And for once we aren’t talking about military
issues.

00:11:53.450 --> 00:12:00.800
(AUDIO: Saudi Arabia Makes Inroads in Iraq
to Curb Iranian Influence.

00:12:00.800 --> 00:12:02.640
- Bloomberg)

00:12:02.640 --> 00:12:07.450
The state-owned oil company Aramco, the chemical
company Saudi Basic Industries and the mining

00:12:07.450 --> 00:12:12.010
company Maaden are just a few of the big Saudi
corporations that are looking to set up in

00:12:12.010 --> 00:12:16.120
Iraq by establishing joint ventures or by
making direct and indirect investments in

00:12:16.120 --> 00:12:17.130
Iraqi companies.

00:12:17.130 --> 00:12:22.140
Moreover, Saudi Arabia opened another consulate
in Baghdad at the beginning of April 2019,

00:12:22.140 --> 00:12:25.550
accompanied by an aid package worth one billion
dollars.

00:12:25.550 --> 00:12:30.320
And that’s not all, because at the end of
October 2019 King Salman and Iraqi Prime Minister

00:12:30.320 --> 00:12:35.300
Mahdi met in Saudi Arabia and signed at least
13 agreements related to trade, energy and

00:12:35.300 --> 00:12:39.140
political cooperation.

00:12:39.140 --> 00:12:46.290
Yes, it looks like the Iraqi government wants
to take advantage of the Arab Cold War to

00:12:46.290 --> 00:12:48.870
get their share and flood the country with
dollars.

00:12:48.870 --> 00:12:50.540
It makes sense, don't you think?

00:12:50.540 --> 00:12:53.470
Now, will the Popular Mobilization Forces
allow this?

00:12:53.470 --> 00:12:57.920
Or will they once again unleash a campaign
of violence and terror to destabilize the

00:12:57.920 --> 00:12:59.170
country for another decade?

00:12:59.170 --> 00:13:03.020
Will Iraq finally succeed and take the leap
towards stability and development?

00:13:03.020 --> 00:13:04.020
Let's hope so.

00:13:04.020 --> 00:13:07.470
So I really hope you enjoyed this video, please
hit like if you did, and don’t forget to

